file.doc:
`fs.file(path, flags)` - an open file as an owned resource.

    var f = defer fs.file("data.bin", "r");
    var chunk = f.read(1024);
    // f is closed when the block ends, whichever way it ends

The same handle also works with the explicit `f.close()` and with
`wait spawn f.close()`; closing twice is not an error.

test.doc:
`test` - the built-in test framework.

    import "core:test";

    test.describe("math", func() {
      test.beforeEach(func() { ... });

      test.test("adds", func() {
        test.assert.equal(1 + 2, 3);
      });
    });

    test.finish("math");

Run a file with `mylang test <file>`, or a whole directory with `mylang test`.

Every callback (`describe` body, `test` body, hooks) runs as a coroutine, so a test may
`wait`, `spawn` or sleep. Each test body runs as its own task, which means a test that
leaves work running cannot corrupt the next one's control flow.

binary.doc:
`binary` - reading and writing structured binary data.

`bytes` holds binary data; `binary` gives it a cursor and typed accessors, which is what
parsing a file header or framing a network message actually needs:

    var r = binary.reader(data);          // big-endian by default
    var magic = r.string(4);
    var version = r.u16();

    var w = binary.writer();
    w.string("MLNG").u16(1).u32(length(payload)).bytes(payload);
    tcp.write(socket, w.build());

Everything here is pure computation: no permissions, nothing to wait for, no resources
to release. Values come in and go out as the shared `bytes` value, so a buffer produced
here can be handed straight to `fs`, `crypto`, `compress`, `tcp`, `udp` or `ws`.

validation.doc:
`validation` - checking that a value has the shape a program expects.

Input that comes from outside - a parsed JSON body, a config file, a form, a message off
a socket - is `nil` or the wrong type far more often than code written against it
assumes. A schema says what the shape must be, once, and every check reports *which*
field was wrong rather than failing somewhere deep in the program.

    var user = validation.object({
      name:  validation.string({ min: 1, max: 64 }),
      age:   validation.integer({ min: 0, max: 130, optional: true }),
      email: validation.string({ pattern: "^[^@]+@[^@]+$" }),
      tags:  validation.array(validation.string(), { max: 10 })
    });

    var result = validation.check(user, body);
    if (!result.valid) { coreio.print(result.errors); }

    var value = validation.parse(user, body);   // returns the value, or throws

Schemas are plain values, so they can be stored in a variable, nested, and shared
between libraries. Nothing here touches the outside world: no permissions, no waiting.

stream.doc:
`compress.stream(kind, options)` - a transform you feed by hand.

    var packer = defer compress.stream("gzip");
    packer.write(firstChunk);
    packer.write(secondChunk);
    var packed = packer.finish();

Useful when the data arrives in pieces (off a socket, out of a loop) and neither end
ever exists as one buffer. The stream owns a host transform, so `defer` releases it and
`close()` is idempotent, like every other resource.

tcp.doc:
`tcp` - TCP clients and servers.

    var socket = defer tcp.connect("example.com", 80);
    socket.write("GET / HTTP/1.0\r\n\r\n");
    var reply = socket.read();
    // the socket is closed when the block ends

    var server = defer tcp.listen(8080);
    server.accept(func(client) {
      client.write("hello\n");
      client.close();
    });

A socket and a server are owned resources: both support `symbol.dispose` (so `defer`
releases them), both have an idempotent `close()`, and both work with `spawn` / `wait`
because every operation on them is a coroutine rather than a blocking call.

Reading parks the task until data arrives, so a server can hold many connections at
once on the single interpreter thread without any of them blocking the others.

Permissions: connecting and listening both need `--allow-net`; a grant may name hosts
and ports (`--allow-net=example.com:443,127.0.0.1:8080`).

ws.doc:
`ws` - WebSocket clients and servers.

    var socket = defer ws.connect("ws://127.0.0.1:8080/chat");
    socket.send("hello");
    var message = socket.receive();        // { kind, data, text }

    var server = defer ws.listen(8080);
    server.accept(func(peer) {
      peer.send("welcome");
    });

A connection is an owned resource: `symbol.dispose` sends a close frame and releases the
underlying socket, so `defer` cleans it up however the block ends. Receiving parks the
task rather than blocking, so one program can hold many connections at once.

Ping/pong is answered automatically, as the protocol requires, and a close frame from
the peer ends `receive()` instead of hanging.

Permissions: both `connect` and `listen` need `--allow-net`, with the same host and port
grammar as `tcp`.

process.doc:
`process` - running external programs, and asking what this program is allowed to do.

    var result = process.run("git", ["status", "--short"]);
    coreio.print(result.code, result.stdout.toString());

    var child = defer process.spawn("sort");
    child.write("b\na\n");
    child.closeInput();
    coreio.print(child.output().toString());

`run` waits for the program to finish; `spawn` hands back a live child whose streams can
be written to and read from while it runs. Both park the calling task instead of
blocking, so other tasks keep running while a child does its work.

A running child is an owned resource: `symbol.dispose` kills it if it is still alive, so
`defer` guarantees no stray process is left behind when a block exits early.

Permissions: starting any external program needs `--allow-process`, which may name the
commands that are allowed (`--allow-process=git,ls`). Reading the environment needs
`--allow-env`. The permission queries at the bottom never need a permission themselves.
